==Icelandic==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|non|is}} {{term|akr}}, from {{proto|gem-pro|akraz|lang=is}}, from {{proto|ine-pro|h₂éǵros|lang=is|field}} whence also the Danish {{term|ager|lang=da}}, Faroese {{term|akur|lang=fo}}, Swedish {{term|åker}} and Norwegian {{term|åker|lang=no}}.

Germanic cognates include Old English {{term|æcer|lang=ang}} (English {{term|acre|lang=en}}), Old Frisian {{term|ekker}}, Old Saxon {{term|akkar}} (Dutch {{term|akker}}), Old High German {{term|ackar}} (German {{term|Acker|lang=de}}) and Gothic {{term|𐌰𐌺𐍂𐍃|𐌰𐌺𐍂𐍃; akrs|lang=got}}. Other cognates include Ancient Greek {{term|ἀγρός||lang=grc|tr=agros|sc=polytonic}}, Mycenaean Greek {{term|𐀀𐀒𐀫|tr=a-ko-ro|lang=gmy}}, Latin {{term|ager|lang=la}} (Spanish {{term|agerio|lang=es}} and {{term|agreste|lang=es}}), Umbrian {{term|ager}}, Old Armenian {{term|արտ|tr=art|lang=xcl}} and Sanskrit {{term|अज्र|tr=ájra|lang=sa|sc=Deva}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|[ˈaːk(ʰ)ʏr]|lang=is}}
